WEBVTT

00:00:34.488 --> 00:00:59.317
And I want to welcome you to today's special topic, which is about archetypes. And this is a very fundamental topic of Jung and a topic I've talked about before, like many, many years ago, but over the years, like my understanding changed and adapted to the topic so that I wanted to talk about it again. And.

00:00:59.638 --> 00:01:23.714
It has a lot to do with human nature and also collective psychology. It has to do with mythology, and we learn today a little bit about inflation. And I will show a lot of quotes by Jung, which can be really, really insightful, and they're really drawn from this huge corpus of Jung. So letters and the seminars and essays written.

00:01:24.442 --> 00:01:39.193
And yeah, it will be a presentation. By me for roughly 45 minutes. And we would start with the archetypes, or as Jung called them, living ideas. And we will explore why he called them living ideas.

00:01:41.070 --> 00:02:05.020
So when you go through all the texts where Jung talked about archetypes, and he has a book about that, even called The Archetypes and the Collective Unconscious, there will be certain characteristics that will crystallize out. That in that context, he talks about archetypes or he mentions archetypes, and he sees archetypes very closely associated with nature.

00:02:05.764 --> 00:02:29.055
So something not necessarily human, but something as fundamental as the weather or salt or the sun, but in a psychological sphere. So in the inside world, not necessarily the outside world. Jung, even though there might be a slight overlap, which we will also talk about. But he says archetypes are living ideas.

00:02:29.215 --> 00:02:41.366
They're a little bit like animals, so they're from nature. They're also kind of impersonal. They're autonomous and they're morally indifferent the same way how the sun or a lion would be morally indifferent.

00:02:41.830 --> 00:02:58.141
