00:02:59.390 --> 00:03:18.472
And a very, very important point that I want to already mention is there's an interplay between the archetype and our consciousness. So we, in our consciousness, we are on a kind of upper part of the psyche, or let's say more outer part of the psyche.

00:03:18.957 --> 00:03:42.713
And when we go deeper into the psyche and the more structural fundamental thing, there we find archetypes. And archetypes kind of get activated through specific images or situations. Something within us becomes active and acts then through us. And this is an archetype and this is what this event will be about.

00:03:43.665 --> 00:04:05.340
And Jung associates archetypes also very much with an image and an emotion. So again, something that can be outside, like an image, but also an emotion, which is inside of us. This already sounds a little bit complicated, and this has a reason, and this is like a fundamental, let's say, problem with understanding Jung.

00:04:06.260 --> 00:04:28.380
Jung has multiple definitions for the same term that he's using. So I went into the text and tried to see where he uses archetypes and in what way he uses archetypes. And this is why I broke it down into 3 different definitions. The first one is really this image aspect.

00:04:28.510 --> 00:04:51.249
This is actually how Jung started to say like, oh, there are certain images that pop up again and again. In human culture when people produce art, when they, let's say, formulate or manifest something, or they concretize something, may it be a sculpture or story or painting, that there are certain images that pop up again and again.

00:04:51.987 --> 00:05:15.601
And the image can be that, for example, of the hero. I have here the picture of Hercules, who did heroic things and got known for that. He did a lot of good. He fought evil monsters and he was very smart and could trick his opponents. And he would grow psychologically also in his journey. And we have a lot of stories with heroes.

00:05:15.730 --> 00:05:34.360
Like every culture has stories about heroes who do great deeds. So this is how Jung became aware, like, oh, this is a little bit weird. We have all these cultures that are separated through time and geography, but they come up again and again with kind of the same stories and use the same images.

00:05:36.440 --> 00:06:01.409
Another thing where Jung says this is an archetype is a pattern of behavior where it says, oh, there are certain just steps, like mini dramas that play out, and they seem to play out always that way. And the example that he gives is, for example, falling in love. This is why I have this picture here of two people holding hands and watching the sunset.

00:06:02.243 --> 00:06:23.609
That when two lovers fall for each other, you can kind of watch it like a movie. It plays out and it has this structure of it. Both don't know about each other yet, and there's misunderstandings, and there's a lot of emotion, and there's a lot of courtship behavior and all these different things. And it looks very interesting and the people might not even be aware of it.

00:06:25.118 --> 00:06:40.576
And another thing where Jung talks about archetype, and this is not so much known, it's really a situation, an archetypal situation that happens in the outside where this triggers something inside of us.

00:06:41.416 --> 00:07:04.063
And the image I show here is the death of King Arthur, uh, describing the death of the king or like, uh, the death of a leader figure that a leader suddenly disappears. He dies or moves away or is kicked out. And a lot of stories we have are about that, right? Like Game of Thrones, it's okay, who will get the Iron Throne?

00:07:06.176 --> 00:07:28.991
Also, a lot of companies change fundamentally when the leader is suddenly gone, very often a lot of people leave because they are very closely associated with that leader. And there's also a fight who will fill out that vacuum of the king that is now gone. You have it also in some fairy tales that, for example, the king dies and suddenly it stops to rain.

00:07:30.110 --> 00:07:49.521
So there's some disorder and order has to be organized again. And of course, a new king has to be found. So we have that in a lot of stories, but this is a situation that also then plays out in our daily lives. And this is something very important to understand. So archetypes are not something that happened thousands of years ago or hundreds of years ago.

00:07:49.617 --> 00:08:07.825
Archetypes happen every day and they happen to us. And Jung's understanding of archetypes is also an evolution because he started with the images, but then he moved to this kind of pattern approach that he said, oh, it's actually kind of a kind of pattern that repeats.

00:08:10.361 --> 00:08:33.823
So when we look more into archetypal situations, it is really this falling in love or marriage or the descent into the underworld, which is super interesting when people go underground to do something, to find something out, to dig through stuff that go undercover like a spy into enemy territory and so on. Or people being stuck in their lives.

00:08:34.160 --> 00:08:55.650
So when people are kind of depressed or can do not see the way forward, this is in a way also then archetypal. Jung calls this the dark night of the soul. And of course, then the hero being then also a kind of archetype that can be triggered within oneself when it's needed from the situation.

00:08:55.826 --> 00:09:04.176
There's an extreme situation and that sometimes people rise up to the occasion, that they go beyond themselves to do very heroic things.

00:09:05.396 --> 00:09:21.033
And Jung looked at all these things, so the, the artistic things, but also the human behavior to find their commonalities and try to understand it, why people seemingly psychologically change in their behavior and also their understanding of the world.

00:09:23.250 --> 00:09:44.921
So Jung sees there this interplay of image and emotion, and you kind of have both to have an archetype. So an archetype is within us, but we want to express it outwards. But an archetype can also be outwards, and that causes something in us, an emotion that causes us to do things.

00:09:45.759 --> 00:10:06.013
This is why he says here in the quote, they are both images and emotions. And one can only speak of archetype when these two aspects are present at the same time. And I have here a picture which is quite interesting because it popped up at a lot of revolutions in Asia in the last year. This is from Indonesia, but also you have this in Nepal.

