00:16:29.379 --> 00:16:41.591
That is to say, effects can be empirically established whose cause is described hypothetically as archetype. Meaning we cannot measure archetypes directly.

00:16:41.979 --> 00:17:02.907
There's not a test, there's not a sensor for that, but rather we infer it by the images and the behavior that we've seen that it matches very well to a certain pattern, just as in physics can be established whose cause is assumed to be the atom, which is merely a model.

00:17:03.760 --> 00:17:11.738
Nobody has ever seen an archetype, and nobody has ever seen an atom either. Of course, this is Jung in his time.

00:17:11.884 --> 00:17:30.106
Now we can see atoms to a certain degree, but the point there is real, like We have the concept and physics of atoms already for thousands of years, and people hypothesized about that without, let's say, the measured evidence, directly measured evidence of an atom, but we could see the effects. Similar about gravity.

00:17:30.700 --> 00:17:50.185
We see the effects of gravity, we see the effects of electricity. Those things, of course, we can measure more directly and better, but an archetype is within a kind of model that has an effect and that can be inferred that there is something that repeats and is constant, same as electricity or gravity or atoms are then constant from material to material.

00:17:50.233 --> 00:18:13.650
For example, we can say then the same about archetypes that we can say, oh, human beings have a repertoire of archetypes. So where there are human beings, there will be archetypal expression. There will be archetypal experiences for human beings. In that sense, it is then like a snowflake. Jung uses this example, and I have it here.

00:18:15.566 --> 00:18:37.918
A primordial image is determined as to its contents only when it has become conscious and is therefore filled out with the material of conscious experience. Its form, however, might perhaps be compared to the actual system of a crystal, which, as it were, preforms a crystalline structure in the mother liquid, although it has no material existence of its own.

00:18:38.495 --> 00:18:57.003
So what Jung says about an archetype, it is a kind of structure, a structure that automatically assembles things. It assembles people, their behavior, images, emotions, and so on in a certain configuration, a specific way that repeats itself.

00:18:57.131 --> 00:19:18.364
Then similar as we have snowflakes, that when we have water and water molecules, you cannot really see the structure that then forms, but when we cool it down, suddenly it gets a form, a very fixed and very structured and orderly form, as in snowflakes. Like there's a certain structure that repeats to snowflakes.

00:19:18.412 --> 00:19:42.587
We say unique like a snowflake, but actually snowflakes follow very, very strict patterns. And similar to that, archetypes. And this is then what Jung tries to elaborate on and try to describe these patterns that are within ourselves and that express themselves in our lives. And they express themselves in a huge degree in art.

00:19:43.053 --> 00:20:01.156
So I already brought examples from movies. I brought examples from mythology and other stories. So it is really that It seems to be when humans have the possibility to express themselves, they do it very often in archetypal way. So I have here several quotes for that.

00:20:02.746 --> 00:20:17.117
The primordial image or archetype is a figure, be it a demon, a human being, or a process that constantly recurs in the course of history and appears wherever creative fantasy is freely expressed.

00:20:18.949 --> 00:20:39.842
And there are no inborn ideas, but there are inborn possibilities of ideas that set bounds to even the boldest fantasy and keep our fantasy activity within certain categories. A priori ideas, as it were, the existence of which cannot be ascertained except from their effects.

00:20:41.730 --> 00:21:05.266
So here on the right, I have the example from Titanic with Rose and Jack. So the both unlucky lovers that cannot really stay together except for a very, very short time, like Romeo and Juliet. We have this story again and again and again, and people come up with these stories. They like these stories and they like to consume these stories. So we have there very much genres.

00:21:05.378 --> 00:21:17.607
We have romantic movies, we have horror stories, we have adventure stories, we have war, we have detective stories. And they all follow a kind of pattern, a kind of meta-story that can be inferred.

00:21:18.360 --> 00:21:38.999
But even though that they follow quite rigid pattern, for example, a love story, they, they meet, they don't find together, then they are together, then they are brought apart again, and then they reunite or they are separated forever. We still like this story and to experience it again and again and again. So those people who can do this very well can become very rich.

00:21:39.336 --> 00:21:59.923
Such as artists, and this is what Jung talks about here. Whoever speaks in primordial images speaks with a thousand voices. He enthralls and overpowers, and while at the same time he lifts the idea he is seeking to express out of the occasional and the transitionary into the realms of ever-enduring.

00:22:02.556 --> 00:22:23.441
So human beings will have love stories from now until forever. And we have them already in the past and they will stay with us. And this is an insight into archetypes as they express. But Jung says archetypes are more than just human. He says, yes, we have them.

00:22:23.505 --> 00:22:48.198
Yes, we experience them, but actually he says animals also have them, and I guess they even easier for us to realize and to see in animals because animals don't necessarily have a culture. They don't have a way of expressing or codifying things for people to learn. They don't write, uh, things down.

00:22:48.760 --> 00:23:13.101
So birds build nests, salmons swim upstream, and they have all this very, very complex behavior, which can only be explained by the fact that they already have this knowledge inside of them, these patterns of behavior, these instincts that they then act out if they want to get a partner, or when they fight an enemy, when they explore new things.

00:23:13.776 --> 00:23:36.611
For example, mice and rats, they hate cats and the odor of cats, even though when they have never saw a cat themselves in their own life. And this is then this contrast in the psyche that we have. We have our own experience and our own life, everything we lived through personally, but in the background we have this ancient knowledge that can be activated and help us.

00:23:37.448 --> 00:23:48.104
So it helps a rat a lot to be afraid of cats, even though that it personally met a cat before. So Jung talks about that.

00:23:49.808 --> 00:24:02.851
There's nothing to prevent us from assuming that certain archetypes exist even in animals, that they're grounded in the peculiarities of the living organism itself and are therefore direct expressions of life.

00:24:03.943 --> 00:24:21.526
He would even say that, uh, plants show certain patterns and behavior that they most certainly are not aware about that look very, very beautiful and that the plant itself is not necessarily aware about. It can perceive itself.

00:24:23.742 --> 00:24:45.048
There is no doubt that they possess archetypes, as the symbiosis between animals and plants clearly demonstrates this, and because there must be an inherited image within the animal that prompts it to act in a certain instinctive manner. So male and females of the animals find together, even though that they might have never seen each other before.

00:24:47.150 --> 00:25:06.055
And Jung sees a very, very big value for us in our psyche that we are there to also connect with this part. Meaning we as human beings have a very complex psyche, which has old parts, which has new parts, but in the end, everything has to play together.

00:25:07.256 --> 00:25:31.583
And this connection to the archetypes and this natural, let's say, prehistoric life is absolutely essential for our well-being in our daily lives. So he says, most of our psychological ills consist in the fact that we have deviated from the instinctive pattern of man. We suddenly find ourselves in the air.

00:25:32.193 --> 00:25:53.427
Our tree no longer receives the nourishing substance from the earth. So you see, when you get back into an archetypal situation, you are in your right instinctive attitude, in which you must be when you want to live on Earth's surface, in your right atmosphere, with your right food. The archetype is instinctive, natural man, as he has always been.

00:25:53.476 --> 00:26:11.568
And he says, this is a very big problem today. We are very far removed from nature. We don't spend so much time in nature and we are experiencing these archetypal situations, let's say, through movies and stories and music and all these different ways.

00:26:12.117 --> 00:26:22.273
But it's a completely different thing to act it out, to feel yourself to be driven then by this force from the unconscious to certain behavior.

00:26:23.967 --> 00:26:45.851
And he says there's also a kind of danger there when people are not aware of archetypes that they can spring into action in a specific situation, triggered by certain images, that it overwhelms the person and they start acting it out and are not aware, and they kind of then get driven.

00:26:46.582 --> 00:27:07.377
I think it's more easily for us to understand when we think about it in context of emotions, when we say, oh, anger took me over, fear took me over. Bringing me to do things. It is for Jung even more complex with these dramas, like when we fall in love, that this can also be in a very, very horrible way.

00:27:08.706 --> 00:27:29.693
And this is where we then have to look out for archetypes and that we can get caught in them, that we suddenly are compelled to act in a certain way, to show certain behavior, which is up But then again, very impersonal. So these archetypes from the collective part, they are very, very old. They're very historic. They have nothing really to do with us directly.

