And therefore on the right side, image from Tristan and Isolde, a very famous story from Great Britain about two lovers. It's a love story. It's supposed to be like one of the first modern love stories. And they are very much in love with each other, but they kind of do horrible things.

00:28:24.944 --> 00:28:47.845
So for example, they fall so much in love with each other that they run away even though he's a knight and she's a princess and he's actually supposed to be married away. And they kind of fake the wedding night of her husband-to-be. With another person so that they can spend time together. And he dresses up in all these different costumes just to catch a glimpse.

00:28:48.536 --> 00:29:06.189
And there's also a situation where he thinks she has forgotten him, so he marries another girl who's also called Isolde because her name reminds him of her, but he does not get in a relationship with her. He does not sleep with her or become intimate.

00:29:07.168 --> 00:29:27.352
And later they run away and live in the woods from nothing and basically starve to death almost, uh, for 3 years in the forest. And it sounds all, let's say, romantic when you read it, but if people would start acting that out, and people do, they're then completely caught and they're completely in love with someone.

00:29:27.817 --> 00:29:46.880
And it's, it's complete madness to act like Tristan and Isolde. And this is what Jung means. Like, you suddenly can become possessed by the hero archetype, or with a wise old man, or the father, or the mother, or whatever. But he says, well, that's not actually you. It's something inside of you.

00:29:47.040 --> 00:30:05.789
It's something that should be part of your life and that you need to kind of experience and act out in a, in a good way. But if it's only that, if you're only that, you will have a horrible, horrible time. Imagine it's Tristan and Isolde, but they're not both in love with each other, but only one person. Then it becomes very creepy.

00:30:05.968 --> 00:30:24.681
It becomes very stalker, and it destroys the life of two people. So this is where Jung says, uh, there needs to be an awareness that people are acting beyond themselves, that they're acting out something which is highly impersonal and that drives their lives and to kind of also damages their lives.

00:30:25.741 --> 00:30:48.846
The fact that's happening there, he calls inflation, meaning archetypes have a lot of energy because they're so instinctual. They're so deeply ingrained within ourselves. So we feel that when we watch a movie or we're in a story or we see in a situation, we feel this surge of energy and suddenly people can do seemingly superhuman things.

00:30:49.730 --> 00:31:13.766
They can achieve things that would not seem possible, but it's not them really acting that out. They're kind of meat puppet of the archetype acting out then this drama and the dramas we have codified in mythology, meaning we can also see the ends. We see the end of the young dying God, right? We see the end of the unhappy couple in Romeo and Juliet, for example.

