This is something that happens, that men and women and groups of people get possessed by an emotion because it's a commonality, right? This is why it's a collective unconscious. It's something that is within a big group of people that in, within all human beings. This is then why certain things pop up again and again.

00:34:50.179 --> 00:35:14.183
Like dictatorships pop up again and again and warring bands of nomads pop up again and again and greed pops up again and again. So we, we need to be aware of that and look out for that and become conscious of that. Otherwise it takes over our lives and we live them very, very impersonal lives. Not our own lives, but the life of an archetype.

00:35:14.280 --> 00:35:36.575
We act out a story which is already thousands of years old, but we are blind to it. And Jung, as he is, he always goes a step further. Everything we talked about now sounded very, oh, okay, it's very psychological. We have something within the psyche. There's something that gets triggered in my psyche through outside conditions, through a situation or an image.

00:35:38.979 --> 00:36:02.646
The question is then really, okay, what are the bounds of an archetype? Is this really then behavior? Is it imagery? Is it emotions? Or is this even that, like a part of reality, even part of the outside world conforms and is assembled by an archetype, same as an archetype, as an archetype assembles emotion and images and behavior. He says.

00:36:04.257 --> 00:36:20.682
Under certain circumstances, archetypes also let things happen outside. And this concept is called synchronicity. Those are meaningful coincidences. And he has written a book about it, Synchronicity: An Acausal Connecting Principle.

00:36:21.878 --> 00:36:44.068
And it's a very complicated concept, meaning that The things that are happening outside can correspond to the psychic situation that one is in. In Greek theater, this is called pathos, which means, for example, when a character is sad, that it rains and stuff, and when they're in a good mood, that the sun shines.

00:36:44.520 --> 00:37:06.729
But Jung sees there that there's a lot of coincidences of psychological development that are not completely coincidental, but seem to be meaningful. And this is what he called then synchronicity. And there he also sees archetypes at work. He says, when archetype prevails, we can expect synchronistic phenomena.

00:37:07.739 --> 00:37:28.681
This means acausal correspondences, which consist in a parallel arrangement of facts in time. So he says, when somebody dies, there's sometimes weird things happening around that time. For example, people could dream of their death, or people could become aware of it on the other side of the world.

00:37:28.729 --> 00:37:36.365
This is some dream and vision that Carl Gustav Jung had about a friend of his, where he then later found out that it was exactly at that date.

00:37:37.750 --> 00:37:54.197
So it is kind of an archetype is felt throughout the world, and this is a really, really complicated topic in the sense that Jung sees their kind of psychic layer going through reality and also they're manifesting certain things.

00:37:54.647 --> 00:38:03.261
So when an archetype is constellated within yourself, that kind of the outside world is also rearranging itself to give that archetype expression.

00:38:03.774 --> 00:38:21.291
This is why you then have people like Alexander the Great or Napoleon that seem to be very, very successful and seem to be— there's a lot of, like, say, lucky coincidences that go in their favor and stuff that kind of works out for them when they then act this out.

00:38:21.839 --> 00:38:40.001
So Jung saw there again something that is not completely a coincidence, but rather this also has a meaning. And that would bring us to a kind of summary of today's event, that what archetypes are.

00:38:40.519 --> 00:39:05.263
So archetypes can be an image, it can be a pattern of behavior or a situation, and it has to do with an expression that happens through us that can express itself then in art, in our behavior, in our lives. And in that sense, it can be then the greatest help because it can be access to ancient wisdom of the past of humanity, how things has worked in the past and they worked.

00:39:06.289 --> 00:39:20.320
Seemingly, otherwise we would not be here. But they're also a great danger to one's personal life, that one's life is taken over by them, kind of kidnapped. And we have to find that balance because we cannot really get rid of them.

00:39:20.384 --> 00:39:37.886
They are really indispensable for life and they're important for our psychological wellbeing that we are connected to them and that we're experiencing them. This is why we love art so much and music and movies and so on, because it touches something within ourselves. And this is what Jung talks about here.

00:39:38.600 --> 00:39:59.839
The appearance of an archetype in our psychology is always a moment of the greatest danger as well of the greatest hope. And I would want to thank you for listening, paying attention, and I hope you learned something about archetypes today and can find archetypes now in stories and movies.

00:40:00.703 --> 00:40:05.199
Maybe even in your own life or in the life of others. This is also a lot of fun.

